  "generated_text": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we will use the method of the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality).\n\nFirst, we apply the AM-GM Inequality to the terms \\(x^3\\), \\(2y^3\\), and \\(4z^3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{x^3 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot 4z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8x^3 y^3 z^3} = \\sqrt[3]{8} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^3} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{y^3} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{z^3} = 2xyz\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{3} \\geq 2xyz\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 3 to clear the fraction:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 \\geq 6xyz\n\\]\n\nNow, we divide both sides by \\(xyz\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} \\geq \\frac{6xyz}{xyz} = 6\n\\]\n\nTo check if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to see if there exist positive real numbers \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) such that:\n\n\\[\nx^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3 = 6xyz\n\\]\n\nWe assume \\(x = 2y\\) and \\(z = y\\). Substituting these into the equation:\n\n\\[\n(2y)^3 + 2y^3 + 4y^3 = 6(2y)(y)(y)\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\[\n8y^3 + 2y^3 + 4y^3 = 6 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot y\n\\]\n\nCombine the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n8y^3 + 2y^3 + 4y^3 = 14y^3\n\\]\n\nCombine the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n6 \\cdot 2y^3 \\cdot y = 12y^4\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\n14y^3 = 12y^4\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by \\(y^3\\) (since \\(y \\neq 0\\)):\n\n\\[\n14 = 12y\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{14}{12} = \\frac{7}{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(y = \\frac{7}{6}\\) back to find \\(x\\) and \\(z\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 2y = 2 \\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right) = \\frac{14}{6} = \\frac{7}{3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nz = y = \\frac{7}{6}\n\\]\n\nFinally, substitute \\(x = \\frac{7}{3}\\), \\(y = \\frac{7}{6}\\), and \\(z = \\frac{7}{6}\\) into the original expression to verify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} = \\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)^3 + 2